---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const bridges = assertion('not-one-state-bridge-is-in-poor-condition')

const replaced = assertion('the-state-replaced-its-bridges')

const replacedSpec = line(
  replaced.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  [{ name: 'State-owned bridges in poor condition', y: replaced.figures.map((f) => f.value) }],
  { yTitle: 'Per cent poor', zeroBased: true, tickSuffix: '%' },
)

// Twelve editions, both owners. The state's line is charted; the county's is here because its
// last term is the one this section shows to be an artefact of a single inspection round.
const bridgeSeries = [
  ['1992', '96 / 21', '21.9', '277 / 16', '5.8'],
  ['1995', '94 / 15', '16.0', '279 / 21', '7.5'],
  ['1998', '94 / 18', '19.1', '281 / 38', '13.5'],
  ['2001', '98 / 5', '5.1', '284 / 34', '12.0'],
  ['2004', '96 / 2', '2.1', '270 / 34', '12.6'],
  ['2007', '96 / 2', '2.1', '271 / 27', '10.0'],
  ['2010', '102 / 3', '2.9', '275 / 34', '12.4'],
  ['2013', '100 / 1', '1.0', '258 / 24', '9.3'],
  ['2016', '103 / 1', '1.0', '269 / 18', '6.7'],
  ['2019', '104 / 0', '0.0', '255 / 11', '4.3'],
  ['2022', '104 / 0', '0.0', '247 / 5', '2.0'],
  ['2025', '103 / 0', '0.0', '242 / 29', '12.0'],
]

const bridgeSpec = bar(
  bridges.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  bridges.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of bridges in poor condition', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="what-crosses-the-water">
  <p>
    Three hundred and sixty-four highway bridges stand in Allen County. Forty-one of them cross
    the Ottawa River — nearly twice as many as cross the Auglaize, which is the larger river, and
    the difference is Lima: the Auglaize runs through farmland and the Ottawa runs through the
    county seat, where every street has to get over the water. Twenty-two cross the Auglaize,
    twenty Sugar Creek, fourteen Riley Creek.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={bridges} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="bridgecond"
    title="Bridges in poor condition, by who owns them"
    caption="Poor is the lowest of the deck, superstructure, substructure and culvert ratings being 4 or less, which is the Federal Highway Administration's own rule. The three owners hold 103, 242 and 19 structures. No township in Ohio owns a bridge: state law puts them all on the county engineer and the commissioners."
    columns={['Owner', 'Per cent poor']}
    rows={bridges.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="bridgecond" set:html={JSON.stringify(bridgeSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Which number belongs to which government is the part worth keeping: twelve per cent is the
    county engineer's figure, not nine.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={replaced} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="bridgereplaced"
    title="State-owned bridges in Allen County in poor condition, 1992–2025"
    caption="Twelve editions of the same federal file at three-year intervals. Older editions carry a second row for some structures describing the route beneath them; counting rows instead of structures would invent a fall of seventy-two bridges between 1992 and 2025 that did not happen."
    columns={['Edition', 'State bridges / poor', 'Per cent', 'County bridges / poor', 'Per cent']}
    rows={bridgeSeries}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="bridgereplaced" set:html={JSON.stringify(replacedSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Two readings used to fit the state's clean sheet — that it maintains better, or that it owns
    newer structures — and the file separates them now. The state's median bridge here was built in
    1970 and the county's in 1982; 57 of the state's 103 predate 1980 against 112 of the county's
    242. The state's stock is the older of the two. What it did was not repair: of the twenty-one
    state bridges rated poor in 1992, eighteen are simply absent from the 2025 file while the
    state's holding grew from 96 structures to 103. Ohio issues a new structure number when a
    bridge is rebuilt, so a replacement shows up as a disappearance.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    The county's last figure in that table is not like the rest of it. Its poor count was five in
    the 2023 edition and thirty-one in the 2024, on a stock that changed by three structures —
    while Ohio as a whole moved from 4.64 to 4.71 per cent and not one of Allen County's six
    neighbours moved at all. The inspection dates behind the change are real 2023 inspections, and
    among the county's own bridges both tails of the nine-point scale grew at once: superstructures
    rated 4 went from three to fourteen and superstructures rated 9 from twenty-two to
    thirty-eight. A stock of bridges does not deteriorate and improve in the same year. The last
    county figure comparable with the rest of the series is the 2022 edition's 2.0 per cent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The county's own site says it has 371 bridges and more than 1,400 culverts; the federal
    inventory holds 364. Neither is wrong. A structure enters this inventory only if it spans more
    than twenty feet, and the rule holds in the data — the shortest of the 364 is 6.4 metres
    against a threshold of 6.1, with no exception. The seven are not missing. They are short.
  </p>
  <p>
    Half the county's bridges predate 1980, and the peak is the interstate decades: 69 built in
    the 1960s and 72 in the 1970s, thirty-nine per cent of them in twenty years. The oldest was
    built in 1909 and still carries Hay Road over the Auglaize River in Auglaize Township — a
    concrete deck arch, one of five in the county and the oldest of them, carrying about 175
    vehicles a day. It is the only one of the 364 the federal file marks as eligible for the
    National Register, and none of the county's twenty-nine Register entries is a bridge. It has
    no name in the record — only a road, a river, and the structure number 0234753.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is also the clearest single case of what happened in the 2023 inspection round. Its deck,
    superstructure and substructure were rated 5, 6 and 5 in 1992, 5, 5 and 4 in 2010, 6, 5 and 5
    in 2022 — and 7, 7 and 7 in 2025, with the reconstruction year blank in every edition. A
    hundred-and-sixteen-year-old arch does not gain three points on its substructure by standing
    still.
  </p>
  <p>
    One caution about the traffic figures here. The busiest structure in the county carries
    Interstate 75 over Lost Creek at 39,060 vehicles a day — a count taken in 2015 and printed in
    the 2025 file, as are the counts on 293 of the county's 364 bridges. The edition year dates the
    compilation and nothing inside it.
  </p>
</Article>
